973:
(a) Every body art studio shall require that each client complete an application, client evaluation and informed consent form approved by the health authority prior to having any body art activity performed upon or to their body. All records required by this rule may be kept in digital or print form.
(b) The application shall contain a minimum of the following:
(1) Name;
(2) Date of birth;
(3) Copy of government issued identification (ID);
(4) In the case of piercings for a minor client, the parent or legal guardian's government issued ID, proof of parentage or legal guardianship through a certified copy of a birth certificate or court order of guardianship respectively, state-issued photo ID or other health authority approved ID for the minor client, and the written consent to conduct the contemplated body art activity to be performed upon the minor client;
(5) A brief description and location of the body art procedure to be performed;
(6) The phone number of the health authority and instructions for the client, or in the case of a minor client, the minor client and parent or legal guardian, to contact the health authority with any complaint, question or concern regarding safety, sanitization, or sterilization procedures;
(7) The name and certification number of the body artist who is to conduct the body art on the client or minor client;
(8) Signature of the client or, in the case of a minor client, the signature of the client's parent or legal guardian signed in the presence of the body artist;
(9) A statement by the client attesting that he or she is not under the influence of alcohol or drugs;
(10) The signature of the body artist; and
(11) The dates of all signatures.
(c) The body art studio shall complete a client evaluation to ensure that the client inform the body artist of any known chronic medical or communicable conditions, including, but not limited to the following:
(1) History of diabetes or any disorder or medication that affects the neurological or immune system in fighting infection;
(2) Bloodborne conditions such as Hepatitis B, Hepatitis C, HIV;
(3) History of hemophilia or any other blood clotting abnormalities;
(4) History of skin disease, skin lesions, or skin sensitivities to soap, disinfectants, etc.;
(5) History of allergies or adverse reactions to pigments, dyes, or other skin sensitivities;
(6) History of epilepsy, seizures, fainting or narcolepsy;
(7) The taking of medications such as aspirin or other anticoagulants (such as warfarin, XareltoT, Plavix, EliquisT, etc.) which thin the blood and or interfere with blood clotting;
(8) History of or suspicion of adverse reaction to latex or products containing latex; and
(9) History of keloid formation.
(10) If the client is pregnant or has been pregnant in the last three months; and,
(11) If the client has eaten in the last four hours.
(d) The body artist must tell the client to consult a physician prior to the procedure if they have any concerns related to the evaluation questions outlined in subsection (c).
(e) The body artist shall inform the client, verbally and in writing, that the health conditions outlined in subsection (c) may increase health risks associated with receiving a body art procedure.
(f) If the client refuses to disclose the information in subsection (c) of this subsection, then the body artist shall require the client to sign a form stating that the client was asked to provide the information and refused.
(g) The client must sign an informed consent form that includes but not limited to the following:
(1) Client is voluntarily obtaining services of their own free will and volition;
(2) Client has had the opportunity to read and understand the documents presented to them;
(3) Client has the ability to ask questions about the procedure; and
(4) Client has received and understands written and verbal aftercare.
(h) For each client, proper records of identification, an application, client evaluation, and informed consent form shall be kept, in digital or print form, and retained for a minimum of three years. Records must be kept on premises for a minimum of one year. All three years of records must be available to the department or health authority upon request. The files must be stored in a manner that prohibits access from unauthorized personnel.
(Ord. No. 11-12, 3-15-2011; Ord. No. 24-02, § 1(Exh. A), 10-15-2024)

974:
(a) Bulk single use articles shall be commercially packaged and handled to protect them from contamination. These articles shall be stored in an area separate from the work area and toilet facilities.
(b) All materials applied to the human skin shall be from single use containers and shall be disposed of after each use.
(c) Each work station in a body art establishment shall be equipped or stocked in the following manner:
(1) For body tattooing services: A minimum of six sterilized needles, six sterilized needle bars, and six sterilized needle tubes;
For body piercing services: A minimum of six sterilized needles, six sterilized needle tubes, six sterilized forceps, and six sterilized hemostats;
For cosmetic tattooing or micropigmentation services (with single-use only equipment): Six single-use pens or equivalent instruments.
(2) A minimum of two extra packages of disposable towels/tissues other than the package that is being used;
(3) A minimum of one extra box of medical grade disposable gloves other than the box being used;
(4) An extra supply of bandages, ointment or gel, and antimicrobial soap.
(d) A standard first aid kit or equivalent first aid supplies shall be readily accessible in the body art establishments and shall contain as a minimum:
(1) 4" × 4" gauze pads;
(2) Benzalkonium swabs;
(3) 2" × 2" gauze pads;
(4) Gauze roller bandage;
(5) Cardio-pulmonary resuscitation (CPR) mouth barrier device;
(6) Eyewash solution;
(7) Smelling salts or ammonia inhalants.
(Ord. No. 11-12, 3-15-2011)

1001:
Application for a building permit under this article shall be filed with the building standards department. Applications shall be in writing, accompanied by three copies of the required plans, and shall contain the following information:
(1) Name and address of the applicant;
(2) Location and legal description of the proposed site;
(3) Plot plan at a scale of one inch equals not more than 100 feet, indicating the size and location of spaces or areas for the placement of mobile homes, travel trailers and park facilities;
(4) Utilities plan at a scale of one inch equals not more than 100 feet, indicating the size and location of all gas, water, sewer, electrical and other features;
(5) Site features plan at a scale of one inch equals not more than 100 feet, indicating the location of mobile home park drives and walks, parking bays or areas, tenant storage facilities, surface water drainage pattern, drainage structures, areas of disturbed soil and existing and proposed planting and screening;
(6) Floor plans and specifications of any service buildings or other structures;
(7) A scaled layout of typical mobile home and trailer sites or integrated grouping of sites; and
(8) Such further information as may be required by the director to determine if the proposed use will comply with the provisions of this chapter.
(Ord. No. 19-08, § 1(Exh. A), 8-6-2019)

1028:
The manager of a mobile home park shall keep an up-to-date register of the mobile home park's residents by lot or street address, and maintain such register of departed occupants for a period of three years following their departure. Such register shall be made available to any authorized person inspecting the mobile home park, and shall contain the following information:
(1) The names of all occupants of the mobile home quartered on each designated space and the ages of minors;
(2) The make, model and year of the occupied mobile home, and the serial number, state and license number or other designation or description of such mobile home;
(3) The date of registration and departure of the mobile home and its occupants.
(Ord. No. 19-08, § 1(Exh. A), 8-6-2019)

1160:
The purpose of this chapter is to:
(1) Promote the public health, safety and general welfare;
(2) Promote the orderly and beneficial development and expansion of communities within the county;
(3) Provide adequate light and air;
(4) Provide adequate water, sewerage and surface drainage;
(5) Prevent undesirable overcrowding of population;
(6) Secure safety from fire, panic and other dangers;
(7) Promote the orderly and desirable subdivision of land;
(8) Ensure the orderly and desirable development of streets;
(9) Promote the orderly and desirable development of public utilities and services;
(10) Provide adequate and sufficient land for schools, parks, playgrounds and other public uses; and
(11) Ensure proper legal description and monumentation of all subdivided land.
(Code 1979, § 2-13-1)

1166:
(a) Submission. At least 15 days prior to the next regular meeting of the planning commission, the subdivider shall submit the following to the planning commission:
(1) A letter requesting review and approval of a preliminary plat, and giving the name and address of a person to whom the notice of the hearing by the planning commission on the preliminary plat shall be sent;
(2) Five copies of a preliminary plat of the proposed subdivision, drawn to a scale of not less than one inch equals 100 feet; and
(3) A subdivision review and inspection fee per lot platted for development which fee shall be as set forth in the schedule of fees and charges on file in the office of the county clerk.
(b) Contents. The preliminary plat shall meet the general requirements and minimum standards of design set forth in article III of this chapter, and minimum standards for improvement set forth in article IV of this chapter, and shall give the following information:
(1) Topography, based on USCGS datum showing contours at vertical intervals of not more than five feet, except when specifically not required by the planning commission;
(2) Name, location and acreage of the subdivision; name and address of the owner of record and the subdivider; and the name, registration stamp and signature of the designer surveyor and the zoning district in which the land to be subdivided is located;
(3) North point, date and graphic scale; and the total number of lots and average lot size;
(4) Vicinity map showing the location of the subdivision in relation to surrounding area;
(5) Location of existing site features; property lines, utilities, easements, streets and their classifications, buildings, streams, land subject to flooding or periodic inundation, railroads, sewers, water mains, bridges and drainage structures, zoning both on the land to be subdivided and on the adjoining land, and the names of the adjoining property owners or subdivisions;
(6) Proposed layout of streets showing the proposed street names, rights-of-way and pavement widths (classifications to be determined by the planning commission); and evidence of the approval of the street plans by the county engineer;
(7) Exact boundary lines of the tract to be subdivided, by bearings and distances; lot lines with approximate dimensions; and the minimum front building setback line;
(8) Locations and dimensions of easements, parks, playgrounds and land to be dedicated to public use, other than street rights-of-way; and land to be used for purposes other than residential;
(9) Proposed layout of the water distribution system, showing connections to existing or proposed water supply systems; and evidence of approval of the water system plans by the county engineer;
(10) Proposed layout of the sewerage system, showing connections to the public sewerage system, where required, and evidence of approval of the system plans by the county engineer and any other state or local agencies or officers having jurisdiction over such systems (approval of county health officer where on-site disposal is to be used or approval of the state water quality control board where private sewerage is to be provided);
(11) Proposed layout of the stormwater and surface water drainage system and evidence of approval of the system plans by the county engineer as set forth in section 74-84, and by the soil erosion and sedimentation control regulations as set forth in chapter 34, article III of this Code;
(12) Any adjoining areas to be developed at a later date by the subdivider as an extension of or addition to the proposed subdivision as part of a continuing or long-range development program; and
(13) Certain other information as may be required by the planning commission, the county engineer or the county health officer.
(14) Any legal instruments, including, but not limited to, grants of easements, declarations of covenants and articles of incorporation and bylaws of the owners association, to be reviewed by the county attorney as required by subsection 74-43(b) of this Code.
(c) Certificate of approval. A certificate of approval of the preliminary plat by the planning commission shall be inscribed on the plat as follows:
"Pursuant to the land subdivision regulations of Columbia County, Georgia, all of the requirements for approval having been fulfilled, this preliminary plat was given approval by the Columbia County Planning Commission on ________, 20_______.
This approval does not constitute approval of a final plat. This certificate of approval shall expire and be null and void on ________, 20_______.
Date: _____ , 20___ | Chairman Columbia County Planning Commission"
(d) Action by the planning commission. The designated representative of the planning commission shall check the plat for conformance to the provisions of this chapter, and report his findings and recommendations to the planning commission which shall afford a hearing on the preliminary plat. Not less than five days prior to the date of the hearing, notice of the time and place of the hearing shall be sent by registered or certified mail by the planning commission to the person designated in the letter requesting preliminary plat review and approval. Thereafter, the planning commission shall give approval or disapproval of the preliminary plat. No plat shall receive preliminary approval unless the land to be subdivided is located in the zoning district which permits the development proposed and is the zoning district which is designed most appropriately for the development proposed. A notation of the planning commission's action shall be made on two copies of the preliminary plat, including a statement of the reasons for disapproval if the plat is disapproved. One copy of the preliminary plat shall be returned to the subdivider or his agent and one copy shall be retained in the files of the planning commission. Approval of a preliminary plat does not constitute approval of a final plat, it indicates only approval of the layout as a guide to the preparation of the final plat. Approval of a preliminary plat shall expire and be null and void after a period of one year unless an extension of time is approved by the planning commission. If action on a preliminary plat is not taken by the planning commission within 60 days of the date of submittal, the preliminary plat shall be considered approved, and a certificate of approval shall be issued on demand. However, the applicant may waive this requirement and consent to an extension of time.
(e) Construction authorization. Approval of the preliminary plat by the planning commission constitutes authorization for the subdivider to develop the subdivision in accordance with the approved preliminary plat and subject to the minimum standards set forth in articles III and IV of this chapter. However, the subdivider, subdivision owner or agent of the subdivider or owner shall not transfer, sell, agree to sell or negotiate to sell any part of the subdivision, by reference or exhibition, until after the planning commission has approved the final plat and filed the plat of record.
(Code 1979, § 2-13-21; Ord. No. 01-11, §§ 4, 5, 11-6-2001; Ord. No. 06-02, § 1, 3-7-2006)

1167:
(a) Final plats of real estate in the county shall be prepared in accordance with the standards set forth in O.C.G.A. tit. 15, ch. 6, art. 2.
(b) Plats shall also be prepared in accordance with the following:
(1) Plats shall be not larger than 17 inches by 23 inches, and shall be signed by the surveyor.
(2) Plats shall conform substantially to the preliminary plat, if applicable, as approved. Plats shall meet the general requirement and minimum standards of design set forth in article III of this chapter, and minimum standards for improvements as set forth in article IV of this chapter. Plats shall be drawn to a scale of one inch equals not more than 100 feet, and shall show the following information, where applicable:
a. Name, location and acreage of subdivision; tax map and parcel number of the remainder of the parent tract, if any; name and address of the owner of record and the subdivider; and the name, registration stamp and signature of the surveyor of the subdivision;
b. North point, date and graphic scale; and the total number of lots and the average lot size;
c. Vicinity map showing the exact location of the subdivision in relation to the surrounding area, and showing streets or roads at a scale of one inch equals 1,000 feet;
d. Exact boundary lines of the subdivision tract; names and locations of adjoining subdivisions and streets; and the location and ownership of adjoining property;
e. Layout of streets showing street names, rights-of-way, pavement widths and classifications, street centerlines showing angles of deflection, angles of intersection, radii and lengths of tangents;
f. Lot lines with dimensions and bearings; minimum front building setback lines; lots numbered in numerical order and blocks lettered alphabetically; and the area of each lot;
g. Locations, dimensions and purposes of any easements, recreational areas, land to be dedicated for public use, land to be used for purposes other than residential, and land for future development;
h. All dimensions shall be to the nearest one-hundredth of a foot, and all bearings and angles shall be to the nearest minute;
i. Location, material and description of any monument and marker;
j. Sufficient data to readily determine and reproduce on the ground the location, bearing and length of every street, lot line, boundary line, front building setback line and block line, whether curved or straight;
k. A complete current mailing address of the surveyor, engineer or other preparer of the plat;
l. Floodplain data; and
m. Such other information as may be required by the planning commission, county engineer, county health officer or board of commissioners.
(3) Each plat shall contain, in such language as prescribed by the planning commission, a certificate by the surveyor as to survey and platting requirements; a certificate by the owner as to ownership and dedication of streets, easements, utilities, parks and open spaces; a certificate as to floor elevation as related to the sanitary sewer system; a certification of acceptance by the board of commissioners; and a certificate of final plat approval by the planning commission.
(4) At the time of requesting final plat approval, a signed copy of any legal instruments, with all exhibits attached, as required by zoning or the other chapters of this Code shall be furnished to the county for review by the county attorney.
(Code 1979, § 2-13-22; Ord. No. 06-02, § 2, 3-7-2006; Ord. No. 11-19, § 1, 11-1-2011)
